  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1230 PM CDT Sun Sep 20 2026\n\nA mid-level shortwave/convectively augmented MCV continues to\nshift east directly overhead early this afternoon. At the \nsurface, a synoptic cold front is pushing southward, with gusty \nnortheasterly winds developing in its wake. As this MCV/mid- \nlevel wave presses eastward, the greatest coverage of steady \nshowers will gradually push east into Indiana through late this\nafternoon. The lightning threat has ended for locales north of \nI-80, as the main elevated instability axis continues to move \nsouthward. Can't rule out a few bonafide thunderstorms \ndeveloping across our far south (Ford - Benton counties) this \nafternoon along the cold front. Forecast effective inflow layers\nare very shallow, greatly limiting available SRH, but deep \nlayer shear values near 35 kts could potentially support a few\nmini supercells. The window for this will be very brief, perhaps\n3-6 PM (if that), with the main potential focusing east and\nsouth of our forecast area. \n\nSince low-level moisture won't really go anywhere tonight, \npockets of light showers/drizzle will be possible into Monday \nmorning as additional vorticity maxima meander overhead. Cloud \nbases may lift sufficiently Monday afternoon to bring an end to \nlingering precipitation, but additional disturbances drifting \natop the frontal zone may result in sporadic sprinkles/light \nprecip chances into Tuesday. Persistent strong northeasterly \nwinds will result in a prolonged period of dangerous swimming \nconditions at area beaches due to high waves and strong \ncurrents. \n\nThereafter, we'll get a notable injection of drier air from the\neast as high pressure settles across the Upper Midwest and \nGreat Lakes region through the mid and end of the week. \nSeasonably cool and pleasant conditions look to prevail during \nthis period, with high temperatures generally in the 60s with a \nvery modest warming trend into week's end. Upper-level blocking \nlooks like it'll hold most precipitation chances to a minimum, \nat least until next weekend when a small signal for \nprecipitation chances may return. \n\nCarlaw",
